    "chunk_size_bytes": 2128,
    "chunk_text": "[4.20 p.m.] \u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0dd2\u0d86\u0da0\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dba) \u0dc4\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dc2 \u0daf \u0dc3\u0dd2\u0dbd\u0dca\u0dc0\u0dcf \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 (\u0b95\u0bc8\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bbf\u0ba4\u0bbf) \u0bb9\u0bb0\u0bcd\u0bb7 \u0ba4 \u0bc6\u0bbf\u0bb2\u0bcd\u0bb5\u0bbe) (The Hon. (Dr.) Harsha de Silva) Hon. Presiding Member, first of all, I must congratulate the Women Parliamentarians\u2019 Caucus consisting of the Hon. (Dr.) (Mrs.) Sudarshini Fernadopulle, the Hon. (Mrs.) Thalatha Athukorala and yourself, Madam, for initiating this Women Empowerment Bill. This has been discussed for a long period of time and I am glad that it is finally going to be made into a law in this country.\n\n193 194\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0daf\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dd9\n\nSo, when a matter is discussed here, obviously, you all have a little bargaining power; men always have their way.\n\nThen, if we take gender equal budgeting, I must say that the Women Parliamentarians\u2019 Caucus has been pushing for that agenda since this Parliament was elected in 2020. That Caucus, I must mention, has also been supported by the NDI, the Westminster Foundation for Democracy, the Advocata Institute, the Centre for Poverty Analysis, Verite Research and others, who came before it on multiple occasions, and has been talking about and making aware, particularly the male Members, about the need to address the issue of period poverty. It is well known that more than 50 per cent of women in this country are not able to spend adequate amounts monthly on menstrual products. What this means is that many young girls who go to school otherwise stay at home on such days because they do not have access to these products. As a father, I know this. My daughter always tells me, \u201cThaththa, you do not understand what it means to be a woman. You do not bleed.\u201d I say, \u201cOkay, I understand.\u201d This is a conversation I have had with my daughter ever since she was 11 or 12 years of age. Over the years, I have come to realize how it has affected them. Like the Hon. Eran Wickramaratne said earlier, you do not choose to be a man or a woman.\n\nIn this situation, lack of access to menstrual products has become a huge problem to some women in the society."
  },
